User: A performing magician has a disappearing act where he makes a random member of his audience disappear and reappear. Unfortunately, one-tenth of the time, the audience member never reappears. However, one-fifth of the time, two people reappear instead of only one. If the magician has put on 100 performances of the act this year, how many people have reappeared? Write your answer in \boxed{} format.

Assistant: Let's break down the problem. 

1. Initially, one-tenth of the people the magician makes disappear will never reappear. This happens 1/10 (one-tenth) of the time, for every performance. The magician has completed 100 performances, so 1/10 (one-tenth) of the people - 10 people - will never reappear. 
2. One-fifth of the time, two people reappear instead of only one. This happens 1/5 (one-fifth) of the time, for every performance performed. The magician has completed 100 performances, so 1/5 (one-fifth) of the people - 20 people - will reappear as two people instead of one person. 
3. On average, half of the people reappear. Since 20 people only reappear as a pair of people instead of one person, the number of people that reappear is: 100 - 20 - 10 = 70.

Therefore, \boxed{70} people reappeared.

Answer: 70.<|end_of_text|>
